rule.description:

A Go-specific rule (no PHPMD analog). Flags data that goes into a function from
a source that is not an argument:

- a read of a package variable that the package changes (a package variable
  that no code changes is a constant, thus it is not flagged);
- a receive from a channel that the function does not declare;
- a read from a stream with fmt.Fscan*, io.ReadAll, io.ReadFull,
  io.ReadAtLeast or io.Copy*;
- a standard library call that reads the environment, for example os.Getenv,
  os.ReadFile, time.Now, time.After, math/rand.Intn, flag.Args, os/exec.Command,
  net.Dial or net/http.Get;
- recover();
- with include-receiver, a read of the receiver or of a receiver field, or a
  call to a method of the receiver.

The analysis uses only the syntax tree. It does not follow calls into other
functions, it does not see effects of methods on values (for example
db.Query), and it does not follow aliases (q := p).

rule.example:

var rate = 0.2

func SetRate(r float64) { rate = r }

// implicit input: package variable rate
func Tax(amount float64) float64 { return amount * rate }

// explicit: the rate is an argument
func Tax(amount, rate float64) float64 { return amount * rate }

rule.description:

A Go-specific rule (no PHPMD analog). Flags data that comes out of a function by
a path that is not a return value:

- a write to a package variable, or &v of a package variable;
- a write through a pointer, slice, map or variadic parameter (*p = v,
  p.f = v, s[i] = v, delete(m, k), copy(s, v), sort.Ints(s));
- a write to an element or a pointer target in any other parameter
  (p.m[k] = v, *p.ptr = v);
- a send on a channel that the function does not declare;
- a write to a stream with fmt.Fprint* or io.WriteString, io.Copy*;
- a standard library call that changes the environment, for example
  fmt.Println, log.Printf, os.Stdout, os.WriteFile, os.Exit, os.Setenv,
  os/exec.Command or net.Dial;
- panic();
- with include-receiver, a write through a pointer receiver, or a write to an
  element or a pointer target in a value receiver (c.m[k] = v). A write to a
  field of a value receiver changes only a copy, thus it is not flagged.

A new value for a parameter (p = v) changes only the local copy, thus it is
not flagged. The analysis uses only the syntax tree. It does not follow calls
into other functions, it does not see effects of methods on values (for
example w.Write), and it does not follow aliases (q := p). It cannot see if an
index is into an array, thus it also flags a write to an array element in a
copied struct.

rule.example:

var total int

// implicit output: package variable total
func Add(n int) { total += n }

// explicit: the new total is the return value
func Add(total, n int) int { return total + n }
